"""European-name passages (Regency, Gothic, modern, Slavic, Nordic). Original prose."""

PASSAGES = [
("eu01", """[[Mr. Ashworth|p|tit]] was not pleased to find a stranger in the library at [[Thornfield Hall|l]]. “[[Ashworth|p]],” said the visitor, “I am [[Captain Hale|p|tit]], and your sister has invited me.” The housekeeper, [[Mrs. Penrose|p|tit]], curtsied. Hope of a quiet evening faded. In the village the [[squire|t]] was thought stern, and the [[vicar|t]] kind. Between them they ruled [[Little Wickham|l]], and the [[Wickham Charity Society|o]] met every Thursday. [[Hale|p]] set down his hat."""),
("eu02", """[[Will|p|si]] stood at the window. [[Will Brandon|p|wl]] had a habit of silence, and the household of [[Marlow House|l]] was used to it. “[[Master Will|p|tit]],” said the butler, [[Hawkins|p]], “your uncle asks for you.” The uncle, whom the servants called [[the Old Squire|t|ali]] and whom the county called [[Sir Edmund|p|tit]], was dying. Outside, a gust moved the oaks. “Say I am coming, [[Hawkins|p]].” Behind him on the mantel ticked the clock [[Old Dutch|i]]."""),
("eu03", """[[Grace|p|si]] brought the tea. [[Grace Alder|p|wl]] had served at [[Hartley Abbey|l]] for eleven years and knew every cough in the house. “[[Miss Alder|p|tit]],” said the new maid, “the [[mistress|t]] is asking.” “Which one?” There were two mistresses: [[Lady Hartley|p|tit]], and her sister-in-law, who was also [[Lady Hartley|p|tit]]. “The younger.” [[Grace|p|wl]] straightened her apron. The [[Hartley Trust|o]] had changed hands that spring, and no servant yet knew the new terms."""),
("eu04", """[[Dmitri Volkov|p]] crossed the square in [[Novgorod|l]] with his collar up. His friends said [[Dima|p]]; his enemies said [[Volkov|p]]; the old babushka at the stall said [[Dmitri Ivanovich|p|ali]]. The tsar's agent was watching from the bell tower. “[[Comrade Volkov|p|tit]],” called a soldier, “the commissar will see you.” The [[Red Lantern Society|o]] kept its papers in a cellar below the cathedral, and the key was an iron fish called [[Silver Pike|i]]. Snow squeaked beneath his boots."""),
("eu05", """[[Sun|p|si]] was a rare name in [[Reykjavik|l]], and [[Sun Gunnarsdottir|p|wl]] was used to the questions. “[[Sun Gunnarsdottir|p|wl]]?” the customs officer repeated. “Yes.” Her grandmother, [[Amma Gudrun|p|tit]], had picked it. In the harbour, the ship [[Northern Ember|i]] rocked. The head of the [[Fishermen's Union|o]], a gruff man known as [[the Captain|t|ali]], waved from the dock. “[[Gunnarsdottir|p]]! Get aboard.” Sea wind snapped the flags of [[Faxafloi|l]]."""),
("eu06", """[[Bianca Rossi|p]] hated her name on invoices, so she signed [[B. Rossi|p|ali]]. In [[Verona|l]] the clients called her [[Signora Rossi|p|tit]] and her mother, fondly, [[Bianchina|p|ali]]. Another [[Rossi|p]], the baker, lived across the piazza, and the [[mayor|t]] could never tell the two apart. “[[Signora Rossi|p|tit]],” said the courier, “which one?” She pointed at the sign: [[Rossi & Figli|o]]. “Not me, then.” In the window stood a cracked mirror, the [[Lantern Glass|i]], priced at nothing."""),
("eu07", """Rain pattered on the roof. [[Rain|p|wl]] was an odd name for an Englishman, but [[Rain Whitcombe|p|wl]] had inherited it with the manor, [[Greywater|l]]. “[[Mr. Whitcombe|p|tit]],” said the agent, “the bank has written.” “[[Whitcombe|p]] will not sell.” “Sir.” The agent hesitated. “The [[Greywater Estate Committee|o]] asks whether you will meet them.” “Tell them the master of [[Greywater|l]] is at home to nobody.” In the hallway ticked [[Grandfather|i]]: a clock older than the house."""),
("eu08", """Two lighthouse keepers shared the rock of [[Skerry Head|l]]. The elder was [[Old Tam|p|tit]]; the younger was also called [[Tam|p]], and the harbourmaster, [[Mr. Gillespie|p|tit]], gave up and named them by their lamps. [[Hunter|p|si]] was the terrier, and he belonged to neither. “[[Hunter|p|wl]]!” cried both keepers at once. The [[Northern Lights Board|o]] sent its inspector every spring. Heaven knows what he made of the place. He wrote in his report: “Two [[Tams|p|ali]], one dog, one lamp.”"""),
("eu09", """[[Elara Voss|p]] opened the letter at breakfast. [[Dr. Voss|p|tit]] was a physician in [[Vienna|l]], and her father, [[Herr Voss|p|tit]], practised in [[Graz|l]]. Both were referred to as [[Doktor Voss|p|tit]], and the postman sorted their post by guess. “[[Fraulein Voss|p|tit]],” said the maid, “it is the university.” The letter named her to the chair of the [[Imperial Academy of Medicine|o]]. [[Elara|p]] read it twice, then folded it into the pocket of her coat beside the silver watch [[Mnemosyne|i]]."""),
("eu10", """[[Joy|p|si]] was the family dog of the [[Okafor-Lindqvist|o]] household, and also the mother's name. [[Joy Lindqvist|p|wl]] laughed when the vet called, “[[Joy|p|wl]]? Which?” At the clinic of [[St. Olaf's Vet|o]], [[Dr. Berglund|p|tit]] was patient. “Both, then. [[Joy|p|wl]] the dog first.” Outside, the [[city council|o]] was voting on a leash law. The mayor, [[Nils Berg|p]], was known as [[the Leash Man|t|ali]]. Fog crept over [[Gothenburg|l]], and the harbour bells sounded like cracked teacups."""),
("eu11", """In the glen, two chieftains called [[The MacLeod|t|sh]] disagreed. One held [[Dunvegan Hill|l]]; the other [[Glen Ardroch|l]]. Both were addressed as [[The MacLeod|t|sh]] at the Highland Games, to the confusion of tourists. “[[MacLeod|p|tit]],” said the piper, [[Angus|p]], “the pipes are ready.” “Which?” “Yours.” [[Angus|p]] was fond of ancient tunes, especially [[The Lament of Dunyvaig|i]]. The [[Clan Gathering Committee|o]] had asked him to pick something merry, and he refused with great dignity."""),
("eu12", """[[Katarzyna Nowak|p]], called [[Kasia|p]] by friends and [[Pani Nowak|p|tit]] by students, taught at [[Jagiellonian Lyceum|o]] in [[Krakow|l]]. [[Kasia|p]] kept a wooden crow named [[Wawel|i]] on her desk. “[[Pani Kasia|p|tit]],” said a boy, “is it true the [[rector|t]] is leaving?” “Ask [[Mr. Wisniewski|p|tit]].” “He says ask you.” She sighed. The two men had been feuding for years, and everyone in [[Krakow|l]] knew it. Chapter by chapter, she wrote their quarrel in her private diary."""),
("eu13", """[[Edmund|p]] and [[Edmond Fairweather|p]] were twins, and [[Mr. Fairweather|p|tit]] was therefore either. “[[Fairweather|p]]!” called the coachman. Both turned. “The one with the hat.” [[Edmund|p]] sighed and took off his hat. The twins ran the firm of [[Fairweather & Fairweather|o]] in [[Bristol|l]], where the clerks wrote [[E. Fairweather|p|ali]] on every memo and prayed. The firm's seal, [[The Twin Gulls|i]], was a source of constant quarrel, being a single ring."""),
("eu14", """[[Sigrid|p]] rowed out at dawn. The fishermen called her [[Sigrid the Younger|p|ali]], to distinguish her from her aunt, [[Sigrid the Elder|p|ali]]. Both were fierce. In the fjord village of [[Aurland|l]], the [[Jarl|t]] ruled by custom, and the [[Hird Brotherhood|o]] kept his halls. “[[Sigrid|p]],” said the [[Jarl|t]], “your boat is late.” “The wind, [[my lord|t|ali]].” Behind them, the rune stone [[Odin's Eye|i]] gleamed with frost. Hope of a warm hearth kept the rowers moving."""),
("eu15", """[[Lena Marek|p]] stood alone at the border station of [[Cesky Krumlov|l]]. The officer checked her papers. “[[Marek|p]]. Mr. or Ms.?” “[[Ms. Marek|p|tit]].” He stamped them. In her suitcase lay the notes of [[Professor Havel|p|tit]], her teacher, and a small brass bell, [[The Little Bell of Prague|i]]. The [[Sokol Society|o]] would be waiting on the other side. “[[Lenka|p|ali]],” she whispered to herself, her mother's old pet name. Rain hit the glass of the booth. The barrier rose."""),
]
